# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from cb import *
from tables import *
import fem
PR = R["prof"]
GC = R["graded_conv"]
V = R["valid"]; CV = R["conv"]; EH = R["exact_hist"]
ex = R["exact_dense_H0"]; td = R["tdense"]
exmin = min(e[2] for e in ex)
femin = peak_abs("H0", 0, 2)[0]
ens = [v["energy_err"] for k, v in CS.items() if not k.startswith("H0_R")]
r10 = V["H0_R1.0"]; r16 = V["H0_R1.6"]; r45 = V["H0_R4.5"]

H1("8. Results and discussion")
H2("8.1 Verification against the exact solution")
P("The reference problem is the homogeneous copper medium with $\\tau_0=0.02$ and $\\tau_1=0.05$. Figure 3 compares the finite element temperature and stress histories at $r=0.3$ and the temperature profile at $t=0.2$ with the exact solution of Section 4. "
  "The temperature curves coincide to plotting accuracy after the arrival of the front, and Table 5 gives the relative $L^2$ errors for the history: " + g(V["H0"]["hist"][0], 2) + " per cent in temperature, " + g(V["H0"]["hist"][1], 2) + " per cent in displacement, " + g(V["H0"]["hist"][2], 2) + " per cent in $\\sigma_{rr}$ and " + g(V["H0"]["hist"][3], 2) + " per cent in $\\sigma_{\\phi\\phi}$. At $r=0.8$ and for the profile at $t=0.2$, all errors are below " + g(max(V["H0"]["hist08"] + V["H0"]["prof"]), 2) + " per cent.")
FIG("Figure_3.png", "Verification for the homogeneous reference medium ($\\tau_0=0.02$, $\\tau_1=0.05$). (a) Temperature at $r=0.3$. (b) Radial and hoop stress at $r=0.3$. (c) Temperature profile at $t=0.2$. Finite element results (P2) are solid or dotted lines; exact Laplace-domain results with Durbin inversion are dashed. Both are computed.")
P("The stress histories reveal the main limitation of the scheme. The exact radial stress has a narrow compressive spike of " + sc(exmin, 2) + " at the arrival of the front at $t=0.0425$, and the finite element solution reaches only " + sc(femin, 2) + " in its sampled history, about half of the exact peak, with the spike smeared over a few time steps and a small precursor before the arrival. "
  "The cause is the limited resolution of a nearly discontinuous front by a mesh of $h=0.0125$ and a non-dissipative integrator; the relaxation length $c\\tau_0=0.14$ is spanned by about eleven elements, but a near-jump cannot be represented by continuous elements and the trapezoidal rule does not dissipate the short-wave error. The errors in Table 5 are therefore dominated by the first one or two history samples. "
  "If the samples before $t=0.1$ are excluded, the errors in $\\sigma_{rr}$ and $\\sigma_{\\phi\\phi}$ fall from " + g(V["H0"]["hist"][2], 2) + " and " + g(V["H0"]["hist"][3], 2) + " per cent to 0.38 and 0.13 per cent, while the displacement error stays at 0.59 per cent. The displacement error is therefore not front-related: it is the regularisation error of Section 8.2.")
P("The limits of the theory provide further tests. With $\\tau_1=0$ (a Lord–Shulman-type law) and with $\\tau_0=\\tau_1=0$ (classical coupled thermoelasticity) the temperature errors remain at " + g(V["H_LS"]["hist"][0], 2) + " and " + g(V["H_CTE"]["hist"][0], 2) + " per cent, but the stress errors increase to " + g(V["H_LS"]["hist"][2], 2) + " and " + g(V["H_CTE"]["hist"][2], 2) + " per cent in $\\sigma_{rr}$ and do not decrease when the early samples are excluded (5.05 per cent for $t\\ge0.15$). "
  "Without the strain-rate term, the elastic wavefront with a stress jump that travels at $c_e=1$, Eq. (@wave@) and the following discussion, reaches $r=0.3$ between $t=0.25$ and $t=0.3$, depending on where in the source disc the front is taken to start, which is inside the window; the computed peak occurs at $t=0.2825$. This jump is smeared by the mesh. In the MGL medium the strain-rate term removes the jump, which is why the errors of the MGL solution are smaller; the observation agrees with the statement of [1] that MGL has a continuous displacement and a single wave.")
P("The porosity model is verified on a uniform porous medium, for which the exact solution of Section 4 holds with the ratios from Table 2 of $\\phi=0.2$ ($P=0.64$, $\\hat k=" + g(fem.Props(phi0=0.2).ratios(1.0)["k"], 4) + "$, $\\hat\\rho=0.8$). The errors in temperature and displacement are " + g(V["P_0.2"]["hist"][0], 2) + " and " + g(V["P_0.2"]["hist"][1], 2) + " per cent, and the stress errors of " + g(V["P_0.2"]["hist"][2], 2) + " and " + g(V["P_0.2"]["hist"][3], 2) + " per cent drop to 0.40 and 0.13 per cent after the front. The same is true for the other limits, in which the porous medium has stress errors of about 0.3 per cent. "
  "A uniform medium made entirely of the contrast phase, with the wave speed of " + g(fem.Props(vfix=0.0).speed(0.5), 3) + " and with different values of every ratio, gives errors of " + g(V["U_cer"]["hist"][0], 2) + " per cent in temperature, " + g(V["U_cer"]["hist"][1], 2) + " per cent in displacement and " + g(V["U_cer"]["hist"][2], 2) + " per cent in the radial stress, but " + g(V["U_cer"]["hist"][3], 2) + " per cent in the hoop stress. "
  "This value is not acceptable for quantitative work, and it is still 5.6 per cent when the samples before $t=0.15$ are excluded; the front in this medium arrives at $t\\approx0.13$ and the hoop stress has a sharp feature there that the baseline mesh does not resolve, which means that the tabulated hoop stresses in the strongly contrasting core should be read with that uncertainty.")
TAB(*T4())
TAB(*T5())
H2("8.2 Convergence, regularisation, truncation and energy balance")
P("Table 4 shows the self-convergence. With linear elements the temperature error falls from " + g(CV["P1_h0.05"]["hist"][0], 2) + " to " + g(CV["P1_h0.00625"]["hist"][0], 2) + " per cent when the element size is reduced from 0.05 to 0.00625, with observed orders between 1.1 and 1.8, and the stress error falls from " + g(CV["P1_h0.05"]["hist"][2], 2) + " to " + g(CV["P1_h0.00625"]["hist"][2], 2) + " per cent, roughly first order, as expected for a quantity that is obtained from the derivative of a piecewise linear function and that contains a near-discontinuity. "
  "With quadratic elements the mesh $h=0.1$ does not resolve the source disc of radius 0.05 and has an error of 21 per cent, which is a useful reminder that the source radius sets a minimum resolution. For $h\\le0.05$ the temperature error falls from " + g(CV["P2_h0.05"]["hist"][0], 2) + " to " + g(CV["P2_h0.0125"]["hist"][0], 2) + " per cent, with observed orders from 3.9 to 7.6, and the stress error falls from " + g(CV["P2_h0.05"]["hist"][2], 2) + " to " + g(CV["P2_h0.0125"]["hist"][2], 2) + " per cent. "
  "The very large orders reflect pre-asymptotic behaviour and the finite accuracy of the reference solution, and should not be interpreted as a theoretical rate. What can be said is that quadratic elements are much more efficient: P2 at $h=0.025$ (201 nodes) is more accurate in all variables than P1 at $h=0.00625$ (401 nodes).")
P("The time-step study in the lower block of Table 4 shows an observed second-order decrease of the temperature error between $\\Delta t=0.005$ and $0.00125$ (orders 2.9 and 2.4), after which the error is limited by the reference and by the start-up of the Crank–Nicolson step from a sudden source switch-on, and the order drops to 0.5 and 1.7. The stress error is erratic at the coarsest steps (6.8 and 7.4 per cent), because the central-difference evaluation of $\\tau_1\\partial_tS$ in Eq. (@post@) is poor when the front crosses the observation point within a few steps, and it is below 0.05 per cent for $\\Delta t\\le6.25\\times10^{-4}$. "
  "The chosen step $\\Delta t=5\\times10^{-4}$ corresponds to $\\nu=" + g(7.012 * 0.0005 / 0.0125, 2) + "$ and lies in the converged range. The cost is small: a complete run of the base case with " + str(CS["H0"]["nn"]) + " nodes and 600 steps takes " + g(CS["H0"]["cpu"], 2) + " seconds in pure Python.")
FIG("Figure_4.png", "Convergence of the finite element solution, errors from Eq. (@err@) on a logarithmic scale. (a) Spatial refinement for linear (P1) and quadratic (P2) elements. (b) Time-step refinement. (c) Error against the exact line source as a function of the source radius $a$. All data are computed.")
P("The regularisation error is the largest single contribution at the baseline. For $a=0.2$, $0.1$, $0.05$ and $0.025$ with meshes that resolve the disc, Table 5 shows that the error in displacement falls by factors of 4.0, 4.0 and 4.0, which gives a power-law exponent $q=2.0$ in Eq. (@regfit@) for displacement; the exponents for temperature, radial stress and hoop stress are 1.8, 1.4 and 1.3. "
  "The expected value of two is obtained for the displacement only. The reason for the lower stress exponents was not investigated; a possible cause, not tested, is that the stress at $r=0.3$ is influenced by the sharp front, which the finite size of the source displaces at first order in $a$. "
  "The finest mesh and time step reproduce the exact solution with errors of " + g(CV["ref_space_vs_exact"][0], 2) + ", " + g(CV["ref_space_vs_exact"][1], 2) + ", " + g(CV["ref_space_vs_exact"][2], 2) + " and " + g(CV["ref_space_vs_exact"][3], 2) + " per cent in the four fields, equal to the baseline errors, which shows that the discretisation error of the baseline is much smaller than the regularisation error. A smaller source radius of 0.025 reduces all four errors below 0.36 per cent, at twice the node density in the source region.")
P("The truncation condition is tested by changing $R_{out}$. For $R_{out}=4.5$ and $1.6$ the temperature histories at $r=0.3$ and $r=0.8$ differ from the base results by at most " + g(max(r45["diff"] + r16["diff"]), 2) + ", which is negligible because no reflected wave can return to the observation points in the time window. For $R_{out}=1.0$, which the front reaches at about $t=0.14$, the differences are " + g(r10["diff"][0], 2) + " at $r=0.3$ (" + fx(100 * r10["diff"][0] / r10["ref"][0], 1) + " per cent of the peak) and " + g(r10["diff"][1], 2) + " at $r=0.8$ (" + fx(100 * r10["diff"][1] / r10["ref"][1], 0) + " per cent). "
  "This confirms the rule that the domain must exceed $c_{max}t_{end}$ and shows how large the contamination is when it does not. The reflection at the fixed boundary is not an artefact of the method but the consequence of the Dirichlet condition; for later times a non-reflecting boundary would be required, which is not implemented.")
P("The global heat balance of Eq. (@heatc@) is satisfied by all computations with the front inside the domain, with a maximum relative deviation between " + sc(min(ens), 1) + " and " + sc(max(ens), 1) + " over $0.05\\le t\\le0.3$ for the " + str(len(ens)) + " cases, including all graded and porous media; the largest deviation occurs for $\\tau_0=0$, where the heat equation is parabolic and the Crank–Nicolson start-up is less smooth (a plausible but untested explanation). This confirms that the discrete coupling of the two fields is conservative in graded media, although it says nothing about the accuracy of the spatial distribution of the temperature. For the run with $R_{out}=1.0$ the same measure is 0.27, as expected when heat leaves through the boundary. "
  "Finally, the time-domain and Laplace-domain finite element solutions of the graded porous case with $n=1$ and $\\phi=0.2$ agree to 0.005 per cent in temperature and to about 0.05 per cent or better in stress at both observation radii (Table 5), with four-digit agreement in absolute terms (for example $\\theta(0.3,0.3)=0.2663$ in both). The time integration and the auxiliary variable formulation are therefore validated in the graded medium. "
  "The spatial discretisation of the graded porous medium is checked by refinement against P2 with $h=0.00625$ and $\\Delta t=10^{-4}$: the history errors at $r=0.3$ for $h=0.05$, $0.025$ and $0.0125$ are " + g(GC["h0.05"]["hist"][0], 2) + ", " + g(GC["h0.025"]["hist"][0], 2) + " and " + g(GC["h0.0125"]["hist"][0], 2) + " per cent in temperature and " + g(GC["h0.05"]["hist"][2], 2) + ", " + g(GC["h0.025"]["hist"][2], 2) + " and " + g(GC["h0.0125"]["hist"][2], 2) + " per cent in the radial stress, and the profile errors at $t=0.2$ for $h=0.0125$ are at most " + g(max(GC["h0.0125"]["prof"]), 2) + " per cent. The baseline mesh is therefore adequate in the graded porous medium, although it is slightly coarser than the rule of Section 7 for the slowest region.")
H2("8.3 Effect of grading")
P("Table 3 and Figure 2 give the wave speed. The grading law with $n=1$ makes the local speed rise from " + g(PR["n1"]["c"][0], 3) + " at the source to " + g(PR["n1"]["c"][99], 4) + " at $r=1$, and the ray-theory travel time to $r=0.3$ is " + fx(tt := 0.0947, 4) + ", against " + fx(0.0428, 4) + " for the homogeneous medium. The computed arrival times are 5 to 10 per cent shorter than the ray estimates for the graded media and the porous medium, and 1 per cent shorter for the homogeneous one. "
  "The difference is expected, because the threshold of 0.005 is crossed by the precursor and by the early diffusive part before the sharp wave arrives, and it is not a sign of an error of the speed formula. The ordering of the arrival times follows the ray times in every case.")
FIG("Figure_5.png", "Effect of the grading exponent on the response at $r=0.3$: (a) temperature, (b) radial displacement and (c) radial stress, for $n=0$ (homogeneous), 1, 2, 5 and the exponential law with $\\beta=3$. Computed with the baseline finite element model.")
P("Figure 5 and Table 6 show the response at $r=0.3$. The contrast phase of the core has a conductivity of 0.1 and the same heat capacity as the reference, so the heat is retained near the source: at $t=0.3$ the temperature at $r=0.3$ increases from " + fx(at("H0", 0, 0, 0.3), 3) + " ($n=0$) to " + fx(at("FG_n1", 0, 0, 0.3), 3) + " for $n=1$ and " + fx(at("FG_n5", 0, 0, 0.3), 3) + " for $n=5$, while at $r=0.8$ it falls from " + fx(at("H0", 1, 0, 0.3), 4) + " to " + fx(at("FG_n1", 1, 0, 0.3), 4) + " and " + sc(at("FG_n5", 1, 0, 0.3), 2) + ", because the graded zone acts as a thermal barrier whose strength grows with $n$. "
  "The displacement at $r=0.3$ doubles for $n=1$ (" + sc(at("FG_n1", 0, 1, 0.3), 2) + " against " + sc(at("H0", 0, 1, 0.3), 2) + ") and triples for $n=5$, mainly through the higher temperature near the source. "
  +
  "The peak radial stress magnitude rises from " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("H0", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + " to " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("FG_n1", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + " and " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("FG_n5", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + " (in units of $10^{-3}$), a rise by the factors " + fx(abs(peak_abs("FG_n1", 0, 2)[0] / peak_abs("H0", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + " and " + fx(abs(peak_abs("FG_n5", 0, 2)[0] / peak_abs("H0", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + ", and the time of the peak moves from " + fx(peak_abs("H0", 0, 2)[1], 3) + " to " + fx(peak_abs("FG_n5", 0, 2)[1], 3) + ", following the delayed arrival of the front. "
  "The hoop stress at $t=0.3$ changes more drastically, from " + fx(1e3 * at("H0", 0, 3, 0.3), 3) + " to " + fx(1e3 * at("FG_n5", 0, 3, 0.3), 3) + " (times $10^{-3}$), which is the quantity that is most sensitive to the grading. "
  "The exponential law with $\\beta=3$ gives results between those of $n=2$ and $n=5$, as its volume fraction is small over most of the zone. The sensitivity to $n$ is strongest for $n\\le2$ and saturates for $n\\ge2$, a result that is useful for the choice of grading parameters.")
TAB(*T6())
H2("8.4 Effect of porosity")
P("Uniform porosity changes the medium without creating any gradient. It reduces the wave speed (Table 3) by " + fx(100 * (1 - PR["p0.2"]["c"][0] / PR["hom"]["c"][0]), 1) + " and " + fx(100 * (1 - PR["p0.3"]["c"][0] / PR["hom"]["c"][0]), 1) + " per cent for $\\phi=0.2$ and 0.3, so that the arrival times at $r=0.3$ change by at most one sampling interval. The effect on the temperature is substantial: since the volumetric heat capacity falls by the factor $1-\\phi$, the same heat raises the temperature more, while the diffusivity falls only as $(1-\\phi)^{1/2}$; the temperature at $r=0.3$ and $t=0.3$ is " + fx(at("P_0.1", 0, 0, 0.3), 3) + ", " + fx(at("P_0.2", 0, 0, 0.3), 3) + " and " + fx(at("P_0.3", 0, 0, 0.3), 3) + " for $\\phi=0.1$, 0.2 and 0.3, against " + fx(at("H0", 0, 0, 0.3), 3) + " for the dense medium. "
  "This is a consequence of the model assumption that the specific heat per unit mass is unchanged. At the same time the stresses decrease because of the lower stiffness: the radial stress at $t=0.3$ changes from " + fx(1e3 * at("H0", 0, 2, 0.3), 3) + " to " + fx(1e3 * at("P_0.3", 0, 2, 0.3), 3) + " (times $10^{-3}$) and the peak magnitude from " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("H0", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + " to " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("P_0.3", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + ". The displacement increases by " + fx(100 * (at("P_0.3", 0, 1, 0.3) / at("H0", 0, 1, 0.3) - 1), 0) + " per cent for $\\phi=0.3$ (Figure 6c shows the profile at $t=0.2$).")
FIG("Figure_6.png", "Effect of porosity on the response: (a) temperature and (b) hoop stress at $r=0.3$, and (c) displacement profile at $t=0.2$, for uniform porosity of 0, 0.1, 0.2 and 0.3 and for a porosity of peak 0.3 that is graded with $n=1$. Computed with the baseline finite element model.")
P("When the porosity is graded together with the composition the two effects combine. The case with $n=1$ and graded porosity of peak 0.3 differs very little from the case with $n=1$ and uniform porosity 0.2 in the temperature at $r=0.3$ (" + fx(at("PG_0.3_n1", 0, 0, 0.3), 4) + " against " + fx(at("FGP_n1_0.2", 0, 0, 0.3), 4) + "), and the peak radial stress differs by about 1 per cent (" + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("PG_0.3_n1", 0, 2)[0]), 3) + " against " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("FGP_n1_0.2", 0, 2)[0]), 3) + "). "
  "The porosity distribution is therefore less important than its volume average in the graded zone, for the range tested. The combined case differs from the non-porous grading ($n=1$, $\\phi=0$) by +" + fx(100 * (at("FGP_n1_0.2", 0, 0, 0.3) / at("FG_n1", 0, 0, 0.3) - 1), 0) + " per cent in temperature and a change of " + fx(100 * (peak_abs("FGP_n1_0.2", 0, 2)[0] / peak_abs("FG_n1", 0, 2)[0] - 1), 0) + " per cent in the peak radial stress, which is the effect of porosity in a medium that is already graded.")
H2("8.5 Effect of the relaxation times and comparison of theories")
P("Figure 7a shows the radial stress profile at $t=0.2$ for the graded medium. For $\\tau_1=0$ the profile has a sharp negative spike of about $-5.6\\times10^{-3}$ near $r=0.35$, which is the elastic wavefront in the graded medium that is not damped without the strain-rate term. Increasing $\\tau_1$ reduces the spike to a smooth shoulder: the peak radial stress magnitude at $r=0.3$ is " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("FG_t1_0", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + ", " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("FG_t1_0.02", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + " and " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("FG_t1_0.05", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + " (times $10^{-3}$) for $\\tau_1=0$, 0.02 and 0.05, and it rises again to " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("FG_t1_0.1", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + " for $\\tau_1=0.1$ possibly because the larger relaxation time delays and broadens the elastic wave so that it overlaps the thermal front (this explanation was not tested). "
  "The temperature is practically independent of $\\tau_1$ (Table 6: 0.2069 to 0.2071), in accordance with the fact that $\\tau_1$ does not enter the speed of Eq. (@wave@) and acts only on the mechanical part of the response; the displacement at $t=0.3$ decreases by " + fx(100 * (1 - at("FG_t1_0.1", 0, 1, 0.3) / at("FG_t1_0", 0, 1, 0.3)), 1) + " per cent when $\\tau_1$ is raised from 0 to 0.1.")
FIG("Figure_7.png", "(a) Radial stress profile at $t=0.2$ for the graded medium ($n=1$) and several values of $\\tau_1$. (b) Temperature profile at $t=0.1$ for several values of $\\tau_0$. (c) Temperature history at $r=0.3$ in the graded porous medium for the classical, Lord–Shulman-type and MGL models. Computed with the baseline finite element model.")
P("The effect of $\\tau_0$ is large in the temperature and small in the mechanical response (Figure 7b). The arrival time at $r=0.3$ for $n=1$ is " + fx(arrival("FG_t0_0"), 4) + ", " + fx(arrival("FG_t0_0.01"), 4) + ", " + fx(arrival("FG_t1_0.05"), 4) + " and " + fx(arrival("FG_t0_0.04"), 4) + " for $\\tau_0=0$, 0.01, 0.02 and 0.04. For $\\tau_0=0.04$ the temperature at $r=0.8$ falls to " + fx(at("FG_t0_0.04", 1, 0, 0.3), 4) + " from " + fx(at("FG_t1_0.05", 1, 0, 0.3), 4) + " for $\\tau_0=0.02$ and " + fx(at("FG_t0_0", 1, 0, 0.3), 4) + " for $\\tau_0=0$, because the slower front, the speed being proportional to $1/\\sqrt{\\tau_0}$, has not yet carried much energy to this radius. "
  "The temperature at $r=0.3$ is almost independent of $\\tau_0$ at the end of the window (0.2065 to 0.2082), since the conduction has become diffusion-dominated by then.")
TAB(*T7())
P("Table 7 and Figure 7c compare the three theories in the same graded porous medium. The classical theory shows a temperature that rises immediately at $r=0.3$ (arrival at $t=0.0375$ at the threshold 0.005, whereas the finite speed theories give $t=0.09$), so the infinite speed of the parabolic model is reproduced by the numerical solution as an early temperature that is small but non-zero. The Lord–Shulman-type and the MGL temperature histories are practically identical, with a difference below " + g(max(abs(a[0] - b[0]) for a, b in zip(CS["FGP_LS"]["hist"][0], CS["FGP_n1_0.2"]["hist"][0])), 1) + " at every output time, in agreement with the independence of the temperature from $\\tau_1$. "
  "The difference between these two theories appears in the stress: the Lord–Shulman-type solution in the graded porous medium has a peak radial stress of " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("FGP_LS", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + " against " + fx(1e3 * abs(peak_abs("FGP_n1_0.2", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + " for MGL (times $10^{-3}$), a ratio of " + fx(abs(peak_abs("FGP_LS", 0, 2)[0] / peak_abs("FGP_n1_0.2", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + ", at a later time of " + fx(peak_abs("FGP_LS", 0, 2)[1], 3) + " against " + fx(peak_abs("FGP_n1_0.2", 0, 2)[1], 3) + ", which is the arrival of the elastic wave. "
  "In a design context, ignoring the strain-rate term would therefore overpredict the peak stress by about " + fx(100 * (abs(peak_abs("FGP_LS", 0, 2)[0] / peak_abs("FGP_n1_0.2", 0, 2)[0]) - 1), 0) + " per cent in this case. The caveat is that the elastic front of the non-MGL models is also the one least accurately captured by the mesh (Table 5), so that the exact size of the overprediction is uncertain by several per cent.")
